---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const calendly = 'https://calendly.com/contact-advised-seo/30min?back=1';
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": "SEO for makeup & beauty brands for Black and deep skin tones",
      "description": "Your customers search their exact shade on Google. Retailers and marketplaces take the sale. Reclaim your direct sales with AfriRank.",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "image": "https://www.afrirank.fr/images/article-beaute.jpg",
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Francis Ndimba", "url": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/about/" },
      "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.afrirank.fr/#afrirank" },
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/seo-makeup-black-skin/",
      "datePublished": "2026-07-16",
      "dateModified": "2026-07-17",
      "about": [
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "E-commerce SEO" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Makeup for Black skin" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Inclusive beauty" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Generative Engine Optimization" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "My brand is known for its inclusive shade range — do I even need SEO?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Precisely because of that. Your reputation creates the demand; SEO decides who banks it. If a retailer resells your shades and dominates your queries, you're funding their growth with your positioning." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is SEO for beauty for Black skin really specific?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The mechanics are standard, the terrain isn't: queries by skin tone and undertone, a strong \"match\" stake, competition from retailers with powerful domains, and an advice need that AI captures." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do I make every shade findable on Google?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It comes down to how product pages and variants are structured, match content, and internal linking. The Diagnostic identifies which shades are slipping away and why." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do I get recommended by an AI for a specific shade?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "That's GEO: a brand clearly tied to its shade-matching expertise, citable content, structured presence. The Diagnostic checks whether you're cited today." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long before results?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Reclaiming your branded SERP is faster than winning \"foundation for Black skin\". The Diagnostic puts the quick wins first." } }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "SEO for African & Black-owned brands", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/seo-african-heritage-brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Makeup for Black skin", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/seo-makeup-black-skin/" }
      ]
    }
  ]
});
---
<Base
  lang="en"
  title="SEO for makeup for Black skin | AfriRank"
  description="Your customers search their exact shade on Google. Retailers and marketplaces take the sale. Reclaim your direct sales with AfriRank."
  frPath="/seo-beaute-maquillage-peaux-noires/"
  enPath="/en/seo-makeup-black-skin/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="hero hero--page wrap">
  <div class="hero-top">
    <nav class="breadcrumb reveal" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/en/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <a href="/en/seo-african-heritage-brands/">SEO for Black-owned brands</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <span>Makeup for Black skin</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="hero-eyebrow reveal"><img src="/images/icon-orange.png" alt="" /> Product vertical</span>
    <h1 class="reveal">SEO for makeup for Black skin: <em>own your shades</em>.</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub reveal">A customer searches "foundation for deep skin tones warm undertone". She isn't browsing — she's solving a problem generalist brands ignored for twenty years. You formulated for her from day one — and yet it's a high-street retailer that shows up first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:clamp(20px,3vw,40px)">
  <div class="wrap">
    <figure class="article-figure reveal">
      <img src="/images/article-beaute.jpg" alt="Makeup products — foundation, palettes, brushes" loading="lazy" width="1280" height="560" />
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap article reveal">
    <p class="lead">You have the credibility and the most complete shade range for Black and melanin-rich skin. But on Google, it's someone else's inventory answering the demand you helped create. This isn't an awareness problem. It's a capture problem.</p>

    <h2>Why makeup for deep skin tones is a distinct SEO case</h2>
    <p>The customer isn't searching "foundation". She's searching a shade, an undertone, a promise of wear on HER skin. That precision multiplies the queries:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shade and undertone:</strong> deep skin, ebony, mixed / medium-brown skin, caramel, warm / cool / neutral undertone.</li>
      <li><strong>Product and need:</strong> long-wear foundation, concealer / corrector, mattifying powder, setting spray, tinted balm.</li>
      <li><strong>The matching problem:</strong> "what shade for medium-brown skin", "[retailer] shade equivalent", "foundation that doesn't oxidise grey".</li>
      <li><strong>Skincare:</strong> hyperpigmentation, dark spots on Black skin, glow, deep-skin routine, tinted SPF.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>"Concealer for hyperpigmented ebony skin": queries with maximum purchase intent, and there are many of them. This is your natural home turf.</p>

    <h2>Who takes your sales while you carry inclusivity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The big beauty retailers</strong> that capture "foundation for dark skin" on the strength of their domain alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Marketplaces and pure players</strong> that crush category queries.</li>
      <li><strong>Beauty blogs and media</strong> publishing "best foundations for Black skin" with affiliate links.</li>
      <li><strong>AI answers</strong> that recommend brands for a deep-skin shade — rarely yours, even though you're the specialist.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The three territories to reclaim</h2>
    <h3>1. Your branded SERP</h3>
    <p>If a retailer resells your products and outranks your own store, you're handing them your own branded sales. <a href="/en/own-your-branded-serp/">How to reclaim it.</a></p>
    <h3>2. Your shade and match queries</h3>
    <p>"Foundation for Black skin", "shade for medium-brown skin", "concealer for deep skin tones": these queries should lead to your shade range, not a generalist's.</p>
    <h3>3. Your presence in AI (GEO)</h3>
    <p>"Which foundation for a warm-undertone medium-brown skin that won't oxidise?" is exactly the kind of question people ask an AI. <a href="/en/ai-visibility-geo-black-brands/">Become citable.</a></p>

    <h2>The pages that make (or break) your SEO</h2>
    <p><strong>Product pages.</strong> A foundation has 20, 30, 40 shades. Each variant deserves to be findable: shade name, matching skin tone, undertone, equivalences. A page that answers "is this shade right for MY skin?" converts.</p>
    <p><strong>Category pages.</strong> "Foundations for Black skin", "concealers for deep skin tones": your most powerful pages, and often the most content-empty.</p>
    <p><strong>Match and advice content.</strong> "How to find your shade" guides, explained shade charts, undertone comparisons. This reassures before the purchase AND makes you citable by AI.</p>

    <h2>What it actually costs you</h2>
    <p>Three compounding losses: <strong>margin</strong> (wholesale price instead of retail), <strong>the customer</strong> (no email, no repeat purchase — and makeup is a repeat-buy category), <strong>the brand</strong> (a third party decides the price shown, including out-of-stock shades it keeps listing). The exact figure — the Diagnostic measures it for YOUR range; the <a href="/en/lost-organic-revenue-calculator/">calculator</a> gives a first order of magnitude.</p>

    <blockquote><p>The what and the how-much, I show you. The how — which product and category pages to optimise, and in what order — that's the work of the engagement.</p></blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
